<!--
  Wildcat Hub behavior PowerQueries.

  STATUS: NOT INSTALLED. plugin.xml 1.0.6 grants 108 fields and none of them is
  on Log or Gen, so both queries below would answer 403 today.

  ===========================================================================
  THE PREMISE IS NO LONGER AN ASSUMPTION. It was measured on 2026-08-11,
  against lapf.powerschool.com, with GET requests only.

    /ws/schema/table/log/count       -> 200  {"count":16987}
    /ws/schema/table/gen/count       -> 200  {"count":1164}
    /ws/schema/table/incident/count  -> 200  {"count":13}

  Westbrook's behavior record lives in LOG, by 16987 to 13. PowerSchool ships
  two independent behavior models and the reference is explicit that Incident
  Management, not log entries, is the administrator side and state reporting
  source. Had that been the one in use here, every artifact in this file would
  have been pointed at the wrong table. It is not. The Incident Management
  vocabulary IS configured at this district (incident_lu_sub_code holds 160
  rows) and is essentially unused: 13 incidents, 10 participants, 9 actions,
  ever, across the whole instance.

  That count endpoint answers WITHOUT a grant, which is why this was knowable
  before anyone was asked to approve anything. A filtered count does not:
  /ws/schema/table/log/count?q=schoolid==1817 answers 403 naming SchoolID. So
  16987 is district wide and its recency is NOT established. Both are stated
  again in docs/behavior-sourcing.md as open questions rather than smoothed
  over here.
  ===========================================================================

  EVERY TABLE AND COLUMN BELOW WAS VERIFIED TO EXIST ON THE LIVE INSTANCE.

  PowerSchool validates a column name BEFORE it checks permission, so on an
  ungranted table a real column answers 403 NoAccess and a made up one answers
  400 "is not valid column for table". Calibrated both ways on 2026-08-11:

    students.zzz_definitely_not_a_column -> 400 "not valid column for: Students"
    log.zzz_definitely_not_a_column      -> 400 "not valid column for: Log"
    log.entry_date                       -> 403 NoAccess field=Entry_Date

  Better still, the q filter path normalises the name before rejecting it, so
  PowerSchool ITSELF supplied the canonical spelling of every column in the
  access request. The <field> lines in docs/behavior-sourcing.md are copied
  from those responses, not from a data dictionary PDF. A typo in the ask would
  have cost a second trip to the SIS admin.

  Two findings worth carrying:
    - log.entry answers 400 "Querying not supported against clob or blob field
      types". LOG.Entry is a CLOB. That is independent confirmation, from the
      instance rather than from documentation, that the narrative column is
      exactly the free text this file refuses to touch.
    - Log and Gen answer 403, NOT the 405 that teachers answers. Both tables
      ARE exposed over /ws/schema/table here. The earlier worry that LOG might
      be unreachable the way teachers is has been retired by measurement.

  PACKAGING HAZARD, still open, read before running npm run build:plugin.
  That script zips the whole powerschool/plugin directory with `zip -r .`, so
  it picks this file up automatically. Verified again on 2026-08-11: the
  archive contains queries_root/behavior.named_queries.xml. Two outcomes are
  possible and only one is now likely:
    - PowerSchool rejects the upload during validation, as it rejected
      SECTIONMEETING in 1.0.0. That would break the whole plugin upload.
      SECTIONMEETING was rejected because it is not a real table. Log and Gen
      ARE real tables in this instance, measured above, so this outcome is far
      less likely than it looked before the probe. Less likely is not the same
      as ruled out.
    - PowerSchool accepts it and both queries answer 403, which is useless but
      harmless.
  The same sweep also picks up queries_root/expansion.named_queries.xml, which
  belongs to a different change and is under the same hazard. `zip -r .` takes
  the whole directory; neither file has to be named for it to ship.
  docs/behavior-sourcing.md carries the exact exclusion diff for
  build-plugin.mjs. This change may not edit that file, so it is escalated as
  a blocker in the handoff rather than encoded as a test that fails: the test
  suite is not wired into npm test or into CI, so a red assertion there is as
  inert as this comment. Section 17 of convex/psBehavior.test.mjs REPORTS the
  live state of the hazard on every run instead of asserting on it.

  SCHEMA NOTES, inherited from wildcathub.named_queries.xml and repeated here
  because this file will be edited by someone who has not read that one:
    - Root element is <queries> with NO namespace and NO schemaLocation.
      Declaring xmlns="http://plugin.powerschool.pearson.com" makes PowerSchool
      report "Cannot find the declaration of element 'queries'".
    - Each <column column="TABLE.COLUMN">alias</column> must reference a real
      table and column that the ACCESS REQUEST GRANTS. The attribute drives
      permission mapping; the element text is the alias and must match the SQL
      output column name.
    - The number of <column> entries must equal the number of columns the SQL
      returns, IN THE SAME ORDER.
    - There is no <summary> element. Every bind variable used in the SQL MUST be
      declared in <args>, after <description> and before <columns>. An undeclared
      :name leaves the query unregistered: the plugin installs and enables with
      no validation error, then every call returns 404.
    - named_queries.xml files live FLAT in queries_root. The name attribute
      carries the namespace, the path does not.
    - ORDER BY may only reference columns that are both SELECTed and fully
      qualified.

  WHICH SQL CONSTRUCTS HAVE LIVE PRECEDENT HERE, and which do not. Measured
  against the seven queries already working on this instance:
    PROVEN: coreTable="schools" (the terms query uses it), flattened="true"
      (all seven), a repeated bind variable (attendance_summary binds :schoolid
      three times), LEFT JOIN to a derived table with COUNT and GROUP BY plus
      NVL(U.COL, 0) (the staff query), COALESCE, CASE WHEN, TRUNC(SYSDATE).
    NOT PROVEN: TO_DATE, in the window predicate of behavior_log. It is now
      the ONLY untested construct left in this file.
    REMOVED, AND WHY: date formatting with TO_CHAR. Three columns on this
      instance already come back as raw Oracle dates (TERMS.FIRSTDAY,
      TERMS.LASTDAY, PGFINALGRADES.LASTGRADEUPDATE) and the pipeline carries
      them as plain strings, convex/sisAction.ts:181 and :206. canonicalDate in
      convex/psBehavior.ts was written to absorb exactly those raw shapes. So
      formatting the date in SQL bought nothing and spent the one thing worth
      conserving here, which is untested surface. The four date columns now
      come back raw, like the three that already work.
    REMOVED: string concatenation with || had no live precedent either, and it
      was being used to build the UNMAPPED_LOGTYPE_ marker inside the SQL while
      convex/psBehavior.ts held the same prefix as a constant. Two copies of
      one string in two languages is a drift waiting to happen. The SQL now
      returns G.NAME as it finds it, null included, and the module builds the
      marker. One unprecedented construct fewer and one duplicated literal
      fewer, for no loss.
  BEFORE THE PLUGIN IS BUMPED: paste both SQL bodies into the PowerSchool query
  tester and run them. That step is a blocking gate in docs/behavior-sourcing.md
  and it now exists to test one thing, TO_DATE. The fallback, if it is
  rejected, is written out there.

  DESIGN NOTES, and these are the load bearing ones:

    1. NO NARRATIVE FREE TEXT IS READ, AND ONE CODE FIELD IS NOT NOTHING.
       LOG.Entry (the narrative, confirmed a CLOB by the instance itself) and
       LOG.Subject (the title) are deliberately not selected, not aliased, and
       not in the access request. A log entry narrative is a sentence a teacher
       wrote about a child and it routinely carries a second child's name, a
       medical detail, or a family circumstance. That stays in PowerSchool,
       behind the access controls that already govern it and the audit trail
       that records the reading.

       The honest qualifier, because the earlier absolute claim was false:
       LOG.Discipline_ActionTaken IS selected, and it is a String 79 that a
       district CAN type prose into. "Suspended for fighting with Jose in the
       cafeteria" is 48 characters, names a second child, and a length cap
       cannot exclude it. So the guard is not a length cap. isActionCode in
       convex/psBehavior.ts admits a value only if it is code SHAPED: no
       whitespace of any kind, at most one '=', and nothing outside letters,
       digits and . _ / + and -. That sentence is rejected on its first space.
       What a shape guard still cannot exclude is a single bare token, so a one
       word value that happens to be a person's name would pass. That is
       stated here rather than papered over, the first sync counts every
       rejected value, and open question 4 in docs/behavior-sourcing.md is
       whether this column comes back out of the ask entirely.

    2. EXACTLY TWO Discipline_ COLUMNS, NAMED, AND NO OTHERS. There are 34.
       The 5.2 data dictionary marks 32 of them "no longer used by
       application" and describes only Discipline_ActionTaken ("the action
       taken for disciplining the student, such as S=Suspend") and
       Discipline_IncidentDate as live. Those two, and only those two, are
       selected here. They answer the single most decision relevant question a
       rewards app can ask: was this student suspended, and when. Deducting
       Wildcat Cash from a child who was serving a suspension that day is the
       concrete mistake this prevents.
       Discipline_ActionTaken is admitted only through the shape guard in note
       1, and every value the guard refuses is counted and reported by the
       first sync. If that count is not 0, Westbrook uses the column as a notes
       field and it comes back out of the ask without touching anything else.
       The other 32 remain excluded, and the test enumerates the two
       that are allowed rather than pattern matching on the prefix, so adding
       a 35th is a test failure and not an oversight.

    3. LOGTYPEID IS NEVER HARDCODED. Log types are district configured rows in
       GEN where CAT = 'logtype', and the integers are arbitrary per instance:
       a published real district has Merits=404, Contact=461, Medical=514,
       MTSS=24018 and a built-in Response To Behavior at -100000. Every query
       here joins GEN and maps by NAME. A future edit that hardcodes an integer
       is a bug that will pass every test and silently mislabel real entries.

    4. AN UNMAPPED TYPE IS SURFACED, NOT DROPPED. The GEN join is a LEFT JOIN
       so an entry whose type cannot be resolved keeps its row with a null
       name, and convex/psBehavior.ts labels it UNMAPPED_LOGTYPE_<id>. An INNER
       JOIN would silently delete every entry whose GEN row was deleted or
       whose id is one of the negative built-ins, and the resulting count would
       look plausible while being wrong.

    5. SUBTYPE IS A CODE AND IS NEVER SHOWN AS IF IT WERE A NAME. LOG.Subtype
       is a String 20, and the admin documentation says a subtype is configured
       as a code plus a description. Which GEN column holds the code is NOT
       documented and was NOT probeable without the grant, so this file does
       not guess a join. It selects GEN.Value alongside GEN.Name in
       behavior_types, and convex/psBehavior.ts resolves a subtype code against
       BOTH, reporting resolved:false when neither matches rather than printing
       a bare code at a teacher as though it were meaningful. Once the grant
       lands, one look at the behavior_types output settles which column it is
       and the resolution can move into SQL.

    6. behavior_types CARRIES NO STUDENT DATA AT ALL. Run it first.
  -->
<queries>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       BEHAVIOR TYPES. The vocabulary, plus how much of it is actually used.

       Zero student data. Not one column here can identify a child.

       GEN is PowerSchool's catchall lookup table. Its CAT column enumerates
       the categories verbatim, and three of them matter here:
         CAT = 'logtype'      the log entry types
         CAT = 'subtype'      the subtypes under them
         CAT = 'consequence'  the consequence popup values
       GEN.ID is the integer LOG.LOGTYPEID points at, GEN.NAME is the display
       name, and GEN.VALUE is the other candidate for the subtype code. Both
       are selected because design note 5 refuses to guess which.

       The SCHOOLID filter accepts 0 and NULL as well as the school, because a
       district level lookup row is scoped to school 0 and dropping those would
       hide types that are in daily use.

       coreTable="schools" is not a guess: the live terms query uses exactly
       that value and works.

       IF THE SIS ADMIN GRANTS GEN BUT NOT LOG: delete the LEFT JOIN and the
       last three columns (entries_all_time, first_entry_date, last_entry_date)
       from both the columns block and the SQL. The vocabulary alone still
       answers most of the question, and the district wide count of 16987 log
       entries is already known without either grant.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.behavior_types" coreTable="schools" flattened="true">
    <description>
      Log entry types, subtypes and consequences as configured at this school,
      each with how many log entries reference it and the date range over which
      they were filed. Configuration and counts only. No student data.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="GEN.ID">gen_id</column>
      <column column="GEN.Cat">gen_cat</column>
      <column column="GEN.Name">gen_name</column>
      <column column="GEN.Value">gen_value</column>
      <column column="GEN.SchoolID">school_id</column>
      <column column="GEN.SortOrder">sort_order</column>
      <column column="LOG.ID">entries_all_time</column>
      <column column="LOG.Entry_Date">first_entry_date</column>
      <column column="LOG.Entry_Date">last_entry_date</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          G.ID        AS gen_id,
          G.CAT       AS gen_cat,
          G.NAME      AS gen_name,
          G.VALUE     AS gen_value,
          G.SCHOOLID  AS school_id,
          G.SORTORDER AS sort_order,
          NVL(U.ENTRY_COUNT, 0) AS entries_all_time,
          U.FIRST_ENTRY         AS first_entry_date,
          U.LAST_ENTRY          AS last_entry_date
        FROM GEN G
        LEFT JOIN (
          SELECT
            L.LOGTYPEID        AS LOGTYPEID,
            COUNT(L.ID)        AS ENTRY_COUNT,
            MIN(L.ENTRY_DATE)  AS FIRST_ENTRY,
            MAX(L.ENTRY_DATE)  AS LAST_ENTRY
          FROM LOG L
          WHERE L.SCHOOLID = :schoolid
          GROUP BY L.LOGTYPEID
        ) U ON U.LOGTYPEID = G.ID
        WHERE G.CAT IN ('logtype', 'subtype', 'consequence')
          AND (G.SCHOOLID = :schoolid OR G.SCHOOLID = 0 OR G.SCHOOLID IS NULL)
        ORDER BY G.CAT, G.SORTORDER, G.NAME
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       BEHAVIOR LOG. One row per log entry, for one school, over an explicit
       date window.

       Keyed on STUDENTS.STUDENT_NUMBER, not STUDENTS.ID, because
       student_number is what psRoster, psAttendance and psGrades already key
       on. Joining on anything else would create a second student key in a
       codebase that already has enough of them.

       DATE WINDOW IS REQUIRED, not optional. An unbounded pull of a discipline
       log is both a large read and a larger disclosure than anyone has agreed
       to, and the district wide table holds 16987 rows. The window is half
       open on the SQL side (>= start, < end + 1 day) so an entry filed on the
       last day is included exactly once regardless of whether ENTRY_DATE
       carries a time component.

       ENROLL_STATUS = 0 matches the roster query. A student who has left keeps
       their SIS record and their entries; this dashboard is for currently
       enrolled students and pulling the rest widens the disclosure for no gain.

       log_type_name is G.NAME as found, null included. The UNMAPPED_LOGTYPE_
       marker is built in convex/psBehavior.ts, in one place, in one language.

       DELIBERATELY ABSENT: LOG.Entry, LOG.Subject, and 32 of the 34
       Discipline_ columns. See design notes 1 and 2.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.behavior_log" coreTable="students" flattened="true">
    <description>
      Student log entries for one school over a date window: when, what type,
      what subtype, what consequence, what action was taken, and which staff
      member filed it. Carries no free text.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid"  required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
      <arg name="startdate" required="true" type="primitive" description="First day of the window, YYYY-MM-DD, inclusive" />
      <arg name="enddate"   required="true" type="primitive" description="Last day of the window, YYYY-MM-DD, inclusive" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="STUDENTS.Student_Number">student_number</column>
      <column column="LOG.ID">log_entry_id</column>
      <column column="LOG.Entry_Date">entry_date</column>
      <column column="LOG.LogTypeID">log_type_id</column>
      <column column="GEN.Name">log_type_name</column>
      <column column="LOG.Subtype">subtype</column>
      <column column="LOG.Consequence">consequence</column>
      <column column="LOG.Discipline_ActionTaken">action_taken</column>
      <column column="LOG.Discipline_IncidentDate">incident_date</column>
      <column column="LOG.Entry_Author">entry_author</column>
      <column column="LOG.SchoolID">school_id</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          S.STUDENT_NUMBER AS student_number,
          L.ID             AS log_entry_id,
          L.ENTRY_DATE     AS entry_date,
          L.LOGTYPEID      AS log_type_id,
          G.NAME           AS log_type_name,
          L.SUBTYPE        AS subtype,
          L.CONSEQUENCE    AS consequence,
          L.DISCIPLINE_ACTIONTAKEN AS action_taken,
          L.DISCIPLINE_INCIDENTDATE AS incident_date,
          L.ENTRY_AUTHOR   AS entry_author,
          L.SCHOOLID       AS school_id
        FROM LOG L
        JOIN STUDENTS S
          ON S.ID = L.STUDENTID
        LEFT JOIN GEN G
          ON G.ID  = L.LOGTYPEID
         AND G.CAT = 'logtype'
        WHERE L.SCHOOLID      = :schoolid
          AND S.SCHOOLID      = :schoolid
          AND S.ENROLL_STATUS = 0
          AND L.ENTRY_DATE >= TO_DATE(:startdate, 'YYYY-MM-DD')
          AND L.ENTRY_DATE <  TO_DATE(:enddate, 'YYYY-MM-DD') + 1
        ORDER BY S.STUDENT_NUMBER, L.ID
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

</queries>
